From 96ea042bf6e9e6580ab97502f4eb91325a21289c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Damien Dusha <Damien Dusha>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 22:01:33 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] Announce: stop byte-swapping the grandmaster clock identity
 on receive

A clock identity is an octet array, and everywhere else in flexPTP the
uint64_t that holds one keeps those octets in wire order:
ptp_create_clock_identity() builds it from the MAC that way, the header's
clockIdentity is memcpy'd in both directions with no swap, and hextoclkid()
produces the same layout for `ptp master clockid`.

The Announce body's grandmasterClockIdentity was the one exception, and since
the print and construct paths were corrected it is the only thing left
swapping. Two consequences:

The displayed identity of a remote master is byte-reversed. Measured on a
NUCLEO-H563ZI slaved to ptp4l on a NIC with MAC 00:a0:c9:26:21:e4, whose
clock identity is therefore 00a0c9fffe2621e4:

  before:  Master clock ID: e42126feffc9a000
  after:   Master clock ID: 00a0c9fffe2621e4

Own clock ID printed correctly in both, which is what makes the inconsistency
easy to miss -- before the print path was corrected it was the other way
round, and fixing the print swapped which of the two was wrong rather than
removing the disagreement.

The second is not cosmetic. ptp_bmca_tick() compares the two conventions
directly to decide whether this node takes the MASTER role:

    bmCI  = S.bmca.masterProps.grandmasterClockIdentity;   // parsed
    ourCI = S.capabilities.grandmasterClockIdentity;       // ours
    ...
    if (master_mode_enabled && (bmCI == ourCI)) { state = PTP_BMCA_PRE_MASTER; }

Those cannot compare equal when they should. It is latent while masterProps
is still seeded from S.capabilities in INITIALIZING, and bites when the node
has to recognise its own Announce coming back.

ptp_select_better_master()'s COMPARE_AND_RETURN on the identity also becomes
a big-endian-ordered comparison, which is the tie-break IEEE 1588 specifies
and is not what it was doing.

Verified on hardware: both firmwares of the NUCLEO-H563ZI demo (ThreadX +
NetX Duo, FreeRTOS + lwIP) report the correct master identity, and the
ThreadX image passes the full hardware suite including a 120 s saturation
flood.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/flexptp/msg_utils.c | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/flexptp/msg_utils.c b/src/flexptp/msg_utils.c
index 828ebe6..ad2973d 100644
--- a/src/flexptp/msg_utils.c
+++ b/src/flexptp/msg_utils.c
@@ -136,7 +136,12 @@ void ptp_extract_announce_message(PtpAnnounceBody *pAnnounce, void *pPayload) {
 
     pAnnounce->currentUTCOffset = FLEXPTP_ntohs(pAnnounce->currentUTCOffset);
     pAnnounce->grandmasterClockVariance = FLEXPTP_ntohs(pAnnounce->grandmasterClockVariance);
-    pAnnounce->grandmasterClockIdentity = FLEXPTP_ntohll(pAnnounce->grandmasterClockIdentity);
+    // NO byte swap on grandmasterClockIdentity. A clock identity is an octet array, and
+    // everywhere else in flexPTP the uint64_t holding one keeps those octets in wire order:
+    // ptp_create_clock_identity() builds it that way, the header's clockIdentity is memcpy'd
+    // in both directions without a swap, hextoclkid() produces it, and both
+    // ptp_print_clock_identity() and ptp_construct_binary_announce_message() now read it that
+    // way. Swapping here made the Announce body the one exception.
     pAnnounce->localStepsRemoved = FLEXPTP_ntohs(pAnnounce->localStepsRemoved);
 }
 
